#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""agent_capacity: is there room here for one more agent job?

Host-side gate to run before starting another parallel agent job. Standard
library only and entirely local - it reads /proc, the cgroup filesystem, the
filesystem and `docker ps`, and opens no network connection, so it can never
hang on a missing or slow network. It prints one verdict line (or one JSON
object with `--json`) and exits 0 when the host still fits the job, or 1 when
it does not. Exit 2 is a usage error.

The gate is read-only and stateless: it never starts, pauses, renices or kills
anything. Admission control means refusing to *add* work; a job that is
already running keeps its resources.

Classes of job
--------------
`--need stack` (default) one heavy Docker Compose test stack: postgis, redis,
    mapserver plus a one-off test container. Default, because most checks
    AGENTS.md documents are stack checks.
`--need suite` one stack that runs the *full* Django suite (--parallel=4, the
    command docker-compose.dev.yml defines). Same stack shape, measurably
    heavier: #1055 saw the container cgroup peak at 12.1 GiB for such a run,
    far above what a targeted check charges. A caller that means to run the
    whole suite asks this class, so the gate refuses a second heavy job while
    the suite is charging instead of admitting it against the lighter rule.
`--need process` one more agent process (an OpenCode worker that runs no
    stack). Light, so it needs far less memory than a stack.
`--need none` report the metrics and the host-wide checks, no class need.

Why the memory metric is not `MemAvailable`
-------------------------------------------
An agent can run inside a container whose cgroup is much smaller than the
machine: on the 2026-11-05 host the OpenCode container and its nested dockerd
shared a 16 GiB `memory.max` while `/proc/meminfo` still reported ~99 GiB
total and ~68 GiB available, and the container ended that day with 153 OOM
events and 11 OOM kills - of the OpenCode server, not of a runaway stack,
because the host looked idle. So the gate takes the *effective* headroom: the
smaller of `MemAvailable` and the cgroup headroom of this process and every
parent cgroup, where a cgroup's used memory is its unreclaimable charge
(`memory.stat` anon, kernel and unreclaimable slab), not `memory.current`.
`memory.current` includes page cache a new job can reclaim; charging a new job
against it would refuse work the cgroup could easily still serve. Swap is
deliberately not counted: a stack swapped out is a stack that fails its own
timeouts.

Why the CPU metric is not the load average
------------------------------------------
Load average has no cgroup scope: `/proc/loadavg` counts the runnable threads
of every tenant of the machine. On the 2026-11-11 host the agent cgroup idled
at ~1 core while the host carried load 34 (1.7 per core) from workloads
outside the cgroup's PID and cgroup view, and the gate refused its own first
stack over load it neither caused nor could end. So the gate reads the
cgroup's own CPU pressure (`cpu.pressure` "some avg10" of this level and every
parent): the stalls this job's future tasks would actually meet. The host-wide
load average decides only where no `cpu.pressure` is readable - the
unshared-host and cgroup-v1 cases the number was originally written for.

Nested docker is accounted for, not trusted: containers started by an in-container
dockerd are children of this cgroup (reported as `docker GiB`), so their memory
is already inside the headroom being measured here, and a test stack can never
be given memory the container does not have.

Thresholds are environment overrides so a job can tune the gate without
editing the file:

  AGENT_MAX_LOAD_PER_CORE      most 15-minute load average per core   (default 1.5)
                               (only consulted when no cpu.pressure is readable -
                               a shared host's loadavg counts tenants this
                               cgroup does not control)
  AGENT_MAX_CPU_PRESSURE       most CPU PSI "some avg10" percent of   (default 10)
                               this cgroup and its worst parent
  AGENT_STACK_MIN_MEM_GB       effective GiB one heavy stack needs    (default 6)
  AGENT_SUITE_MIN_MEM_GB       effective GiB a `--need suite` stack   (default 8)
                               needs: the full-suite arm of #1055, heavier
                               than a targeted check
  AGENT_PROCESS_MIN_MEM_GB     effective GiB one agent process needs  (default 2)
  AGENT_MIN_MEM_GB             floor for a stack when no memory limit (default 16)
                               of this cgroup is visible (the host case)
  AGENT_MEM_RESERVE_GB         GiB kept out of reach, always          (default 2)
  AGENT_MEM_NEAR_CEILING_PCT   refuse stacks at/above this % of the   (default 90)
                               cgroup limit charged to unreclaimable
                               memory - the recovery hysteresis
  AGENT_MAX_MEM_PRESSURE       most memory PSI "some avg10" percent   (default 10)
                               for a new stack
  AGENT_MIN_DISK_GB            least free disk on /, in GiB           (default 100)
  AGENT_MAX_STACKS             most running mrmap-agent-*/mrmap-ao-*  (default 3)
                               stacks
  AGENT_MAX_PROCESSES          most running agent processes           (default 6)
  AGENT_PROCESS_MATCH          process name to count as an agent      (default opencode)

Test and integration seams: `AGENT_CGROUP_ROOT` and `AGENT_PROC_ROOT` point the
metrics at another filesystem root (offline tests fabricate a cgroup),
`--stacks` and `--processes` pass counts a caller already knows (a supervisor
that tracks its own jobs), `--json` gives the machine-readable form and
`evaluate()` is importable for callers that would rather not fork. `--wait N`
turns a refusal into a queue: the gate re-reads its metrics for up to N seconds
and exits when a verdict says fits, so a caller that can afford to be late does
not have to poll the gate itself - waiting is still admission control, a running
job is never displaced for the newcomer.

Counting is *prospective*: the reported stack and process counts are the ones
already running, and the job being decided about is added before comparing with
the maximum. So `stacks 3 (max 3)` refuses a fourth - the count that fits is
`stacks 2 (max 3)`.
"""

import argparse
import json
import os
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import time

GB = 1024 ** 3
# An agent Compose stack is named after its job. The repository convention is
# mrmap-agent-<issue>-<run>; an Agent Orchestrator host generates
# mrmap-ao-<worktree-hash> per worktree (its wrapper pins it in .env and checks
# it before every run). Both name agent stacks, so both are counted; anything
# else - a developer's own project among them - is not an agent stack and the
# caller that knows better passes --stacks.
STACK_PREFIXES = ("mrmap-agent-", "mrmap-ao-")
PROJECT_LABEL = "com.docker.compose.project"
CGROUP_ROOT = os.environ.get("AGENT_CGROUP_ROOT", "/sys/fs/cgroup")
PROC_ROOT = os.environ.get("AGENT_PROC_ROOT", "/proc")
# memory.stat keys that a new job cannot reclaim away: anonymous memory, kernel
# charges and unreclaimable slab. "file" and slab_reclaimable are deliberately
# left out - they are exactly the memory a new allocation evicts.
COMMITTED_KEYS = ("anon", "slab_unreclaimable")
COMMITTED_KERNEL_KEYS = ("kernel_stack", "pagetables", "sec_pagetables",
                         "percpu", "vmalloc")


def read_text(path):
    """Contents of a pseudo file, or None if it is not there or unreadable."""
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            return f.read()
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None


def read_int(path):
    text = read_text(path)
    if text is None:
        return None
    try:
        return int(text.strip())
    except ValueError:
        return None


def read_bytes(path):
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            return f.read()
    except OSError:
        return None


def parse_limit(text):
    """A cgroup limit value: bytes, or None for the literal "max"."""
    if text is None:
        return None
    text = text.strip()
    if text in ("", "max"):
        return None
    try:
        return int(text)
    except ValueError:
        return None


def load_per_core(proc_root=PROC_ROOT):
    """15-minute load average divided by core count, or None if unreadable."""
    text = read_text(os.path.join(proc_root, "loadavg"))
    try:
        load15 = float(text.split()[2])
    except (AttributeError, IndexError, ValueError):
        return None
    cores = os.cpu_count() or 1
    return load15 / cores


def mem_available_gb(proc_root=PROC_ROOT):
    """MemAvailable from /proc/meminfo in GiB, or None if unreadable.

    Host-wide, so inside a container with a memory limit this is the machine's
    number, not the container's - see the module docstring for why using it
    alone got the 2026-11-05 host OOM-killed.
    """
    text = read_text(os.path.join(proc_root, "meminfo"))
    if text is None:
        return None
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("MemAvailable:"):
            try:
                return int(line.split()[1]) * 1024 / GB
            except (IndexError, ValueError):
                return None
    return None


def disk_free_gb():
    """Free bytes on / in GiB, or None if the filesystem cannot be stat'd."""
    try:
        return shutil.disk_usage("/").free / GB
    except OSError:
        return None


def self_cgroup_path(proc_root=PROC_ROOT):
    """cgroup-v2 path of this process, "/" for a namespace root, None if unknown."""
    text = read_text(os.path.join(proc_root, "self", "cgroup"))
    if text is None:
        return None
    for line in text.splitlines():
        parts = line.split("::")
        if len(parts) == 2 and parts[0] == "0":
            return parts[1].strip() or "/"
    return None


def cgroup_levels(proc_root=PROC_ROOT, cgroup_root=CGROUP_ROOT):
    """Directories of this cgroup and every parent, leaf first.

    Walking to the root is what catches a limit set above the container: a
    container can be unlimited inside and still be confined by the slice the
    runtime itself runs in, and the smallest headroom is the one that OOMs.
    """
    path = self_cgroup_path(proc_root)
    if path is None:
        return []
    levels, seen = [], set()
    cur = path
    while True:
        # lstrip: an absolute cgroup path would otherwise swallow the root in
        # os.path.join and the metrics would silently read nothing.
        d = os.path.normpath(os.path.join(cgroup_root, cur.lstrip("/")))
        if d not in seen:
            levels.append((cur if cur else "/", d))
            seen.add(d)
        if cur in ("", "/"):
            break
        cur = os.path.dirname(cur.rstrip("/")) or "/"
    return levels


def parse_kv(text):
    """Parse "key value" lines into a dict of ints; {} when unreadable."""
    out = {}
    if text is None:
        return out
    for line in text.splitlines():
        parts = line.split()
        if len(parts) != 2:
            continue
        try:
            out[parts[0]] = int(parts[1])
        except ValueError:
            continue
    return out


def psi_some_avg10(path):
    """PSI "some avg10" percentage from a pressure file, or None."""
    text = read_text(path)
    if text is None:
        return None
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("some"):
            for field in line.split():
                if field.startswith("avg10="):
                    try:
                        return float(field.split("=", 1)[1])
                    except ValueError:
                        return None
    return None


def cgroup_cpu_pressure(proc_root=PROC_ROOT, cgroup_root=CGROUP_ROOT):
    """CPU PSI "some avg10" of this cgroup and its parents, worst level.

    On a host shared with workloads outside this cgroup, `/proc/loadavg`
    counts the runnable threads of every tenant - there is no cgroup-scoped
    load average to read - and a gate built on it refuses its own idle jobs
    over load the jobs did not cause and cannot end. The cgroup's own stall
    percentage is the room a new job would actually meet. The worst of this
    level and every parent answers, because a stalling parent stalls every
    task below it. None when no `cpu.pressure` is readable (cgroup v1, no
    PSI), where the caller falls back to the host-wide load average.
    """
    worst = None
    for _rel, d in cgroup_levels(proc_root, cgroup_root):
        p = psi_some_avg10(os.path.join(d, "cpu.pressure"))
        if p is not None and (worst is None or p > worst):
            worst = p
    return worst


def committed_bytes(stat_path):
    """Unreclaimable charge of a cgroup from memory.stat, or None without it.

    The kernel documents `kernel` as already covering the kernel_stack,
    pagetables, percpu and vmalloc charges on some versions and not on others,
    so the kernel side is taken as the larger of the aggregate and the sum of
    its parts instead of the sum of both, which would over-count.
    """
    stat = parse_kv(read_text(stat_path))
    if not stat:
        return None
    try:
        total = sum(stat.get(k, 0) for k in COMMITTED_KEYS)
        parts = sum(stat.get(k, 0) for k in COMMITTED_KERNEL_KEYS)
        return total + max(stat.get("kernel", 0), parts)
    except TypeError:
        return None


def cgroup_memory(proc_root=PROC_ROOT, cgroup_root=CGROUP_ROOT):
    """Memory of this cgroup and its parents.

    Returns a dict with: headroom_gb (effective, smallest across levels),
    limit_gb, current_gb, committed_gb, peak_gb, used_pct, path (the binding
    level), pressure (memory PSI some avg10), oom (memory.events), limited
    (False when no level in sight caps memory) and available (False when no
    cgroup file could be read at all, i.e. an unrestricted host).
    """
    out = {"available": False, "limited": False, "headroom_gb": None,
           "limit_gb": None, "current_gb": None, "committed_gb": None,
           "peak_gb": None, "used_pct": None, "path": None, "pressure": None,
           "oom": {}, "nested_gb": None}
    best = None
    diag = None
    for rel, d in cgroup_levels(proc_root, cgroup_root):
        limit = parse_limit(read_text(os.path.join(d, "memory.max")))
        current = read_int(os.path.join(d, "memory.current"))
        committed = committed_bytes(os.path.join(d, "memory.stat"))
        if limit is None and current is None and committed is None:
            continue
        out["available"] = True
        if diag is None:
            diag = (rel, d, limit, current, committed)
        used = committed if committed is not None else current
        headroom = None
        if limit is not None and used is not None:
            headroom = max(0, limit - used)
        if headroom is not None and (best is None or headroom < best[0]):
            best = (headroom, rel, d, limit, current, committed)
    if best is None:
        # Nothing in sight caps memory: the unrestricted host case, where the
        # host's own MemAvailable is the right metric. Keep the diagnostics of
        # the level we could actually read.
        if diag is not None:
            rel, d, limit, current, committed = diag
            out.update({"path": rel, "limit_gb": None if limit is None else limit / GB,
                        "current_gb": None if current is None else current / GB,
                        "committed_gb": None if committed is None else committed / GB})
            peak = read_int(os.path.join(d, "memory.peak"))
            out["peak_gb"] = None if peak is None else peak / GB
            out["oom"] = parse_kv(read_text(os.path.join(d, "memory.events")))
            out["pressure"] = psi_some_avg10(os.path.join(d, "memory.pressure"))
        return _nested_docker(out, cgroup_root)
    headroom, rel, d, limit, current, committed = best
    out.update({
        "limited": True,
        "headroom_gb": headroom / GB,
        "path": rel,
        "limit_gb": limit / GB,
        "current_gb": None if current is None else current / GB,
        "committed_gb": None if committed is None else committed / GB,
    })
    peak = read_int(os.path.join(d, "memory.peak"))
    out["peak_gb"] = None if peak is None else peak / GB
    out["oom"] = parse_kv(read_text(os.path.join(d, "memory.events")))
    out["pressure"] = psi_some_avg10(os.path.join(d, "memory.pressure"))
    if committed is not None and limit:
        out["used_pct"] = 100.0 * committed / limit
    return _nested_docker(out, cgroup_root)


def _nested_docker(out, cgroup_root):
    """Memory an in-container dockerd currently holds, for diagnostics.

    Those containers are children of this cgroup, so their memory is already
    inside headroom_gb; the number is reported to explain where it went.
    """
    for name in ("docker", "docker.service"):
        cur = read_int(os.path.join(cgroup_root, name, "memory.current"))
        if cur is not None:
            out["nested_gb"] = cur / GB
            break
    return out


def count_stacks(projects):
    """How many of the compose project names belong to agent stacks."""
    return len([p for p in projects
                if any(p.startswith(prefix) for prefix in STACK_PREFIXES)])


def agent_stacks():
    """Running agent Compose stacks, or None if docker is unusable.

    Counted by compose project (one per agent job), not per container, so the
    limit reads in stacks the way AGENT_MAX_STACKS says. A fixed timeout keeps a
    wedged docker daemon from blocking the check.
    """
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(
            ["docker", "ps", "--format", '{{.Label "%s"}}' % PROJECT_LABEL],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None
    projects = {ln.strip() for ln in (r.stdout or "").splitlines() if ln.strip()}
    return count_stacks(projects)


def agent_processes(proc_root=PROC_ROOT, match=None):
    """Running agent processes, or None if /proc cannot be read.

    A cheap, network-free proxy for "how many workers are on this host": the
    number of processes whose argv[0] is the agent executable. A supervisor
    that tracks its own workers passes --processes and does not rely on this.
    """
    match = match or os.environ.get("AGENT_PROCESS_MATCH", "opencode")
    try:
        entries = [p for p in os.listdir(proc_root) if p.isdigit()]
    except OSError:
        return None
    me = str(os.getpid())
    count = 0
    for pid in entries:
        if pid == me:
            continue
        raw = read_bytes(os.path.join(proc_root, pid, "cmdline"))
        if not raw:
            continue
        argv = raw.split(b"\0")
        if not argv or not argv[0]:
            continue
        if os.path.basename(argv[0].decode("utf-8", "replace")) == match:
            count += 1
    return count


def pids(cgroup_root=CGROUP_ROOT):
    """pids.current and pids.max of this cgroup, for diagnostics only.

    The 2026-11-05 diagnosis recorded a PID peak far below the limit, so the
    gate reports this and does not gate on it; a PID shortage looks like a
    docker failure long before it looks like a memory problem.
    """
    cur = read_int(os.path.join(cgroup_root, "pids.current"))
    max_ = parse_limit(read_text(os.path.join(cgroup_root, "pids.max")))
    if cur is None and max_ is None:
        return None
    return {"current": cur, "max": max_}


def fmt(v):
    return "n/a" if v is None else f"{v:.1f}"


def evaluate(need="stack", stacks=None, processes=None, env=None,
             proc_root=PROC_ROOT, cgroup_root=CGROUP_ROOT):
    """Decide one admission. Returns a plain dict; the only source of truth.

    `need` is the class of the job being started, `stacks` and `processes` the
    counts that are already running (None: measure them here). Counts are
    prospective: this job is added before comparing with the maxima.
    `env`, `proc_root` and `cgroup_root` are the seams the offline tests use to
    decide thresholds and metrics without a real cgroup.
    """
    e = os.environ if env is None else env

    def threshold(name, default):
        try:
            return float(e.get(name, "").strip())
        except ValueError:
            return default

    max_load = threshold("AGENT_MAX_LOAD_PER_CORE", 1.5)
    min_disk = threshold("AGENT_MIN_DISK_GB", 100)
    max_stacks = threshold("AGENT_MAX_STACKS", 3)
    max_processes = threshold("AGENT_MAX_PROCESSES", 6)
    stack_need = threshold("AGENT_STACK_MIN_MEM_GB", 6)
    suite_need = threshold("AGENT_SUITE_MIN_MEM_GB", 8)
    process_need = threshold("AGENT_PROCESS_MIN_MEM_GB", 2)
    host_floor = threshold("AGENT_MIN_MEM_GB", 16)
    reserve = threshold("AGENT_MEM_RESERVE_GB", 2)
    near_ceiling = threshold("AGENT_MEM_NEAR_CEILING_PCT", 90)
    max_pressure = threshold("AGENT_MAX_MEM_PRESSURE", 10)
    max_cpu_pressure = threshold("AGENT_MAX_CPU_PRESSURE", 10)

    load = load_per_core(proc_root)
    cpu_pressure = cgroup_cpu_pressure(proc_root, cgroup_root)
    host_mem = mem_available_gb(proc_root)
    disk = disk_free_gb()
    cg = cgroup_memory(proc_root, cgroup_root)
    if stacks is None:
        stacks = agent_stacks()
    if processes is None and need == "process":
        processes = agent_processes(proc_root)

    # Effective headroom: the tighter of the machine's free memory and the
    # cgroup this job would be born into, minus the reserve.
    candidates = [v for v in (host_mem, cg["headroom_gb"]) if v is not None]
    effective = (min(candidates) - reserve) if candidates else None

    if need == "stack":
        required = stack_need if cg["limited"] else max(stack_need, host_floor)
    elif need == "suite":
        required = suite_need if cg["limited"] else max(suite_need, host_floor)
    elif need == "process":
        required = process_need
    else:
        required = 0.0

    problems = []
    # CPU admission reads the cgroup's own stall signal where it exists: on a
    # host shared with tenants outside this cgroup /proc/loadavg counts all of
    # them, and refusing over load this cgroup neither causes nor can end
    # starves the jobs the gate exists to protect. The host-wide load average
    # is the fallback for cgroup trees without CPU PSI.
    if cpu_pressure is not None:
        if cpu_pressure > max_cpu_pressure:
            problems.append("cpu")
    elif load is not None and load > max_load:
        problems.append("load")
    if effective is not None and effective < required:
        problems.append("mem")
    if disk is not None and disk < min_disk:
        problems.append("disk")
    if need in ("stack", "suite"):
        # Under pressure or at the ceiling the cgroup is already thrashing;
        # a new stack would be the one that OOMs. Recovery is automatic: as
        # soon as the charge or the pressure drops the verdict flips.
        if cg["pressure"] is not None and cg["pressure"] > max_pressure:
            problems.append("pressure")
        if cg["limited"] and cg["used_pct"] is not None and cg["used_pct"] >= near_ceiling:
            problems.append("ceiling")
        if stacks is not None and stacks + 1 > max_stacks:
            problems.append("stacks")
    if need == "process" and processes is not None and processes + 1 > max_processes:
        problems.append("processes")

    return {
        "need": need,
        "fits": not problems,
        "reasons": problems,
        "metrics": {
            "load_per_core": load, "load_max": max_load,
            "cpu_pressure_avg10": cpu_pressure,
            "cpu_pressure_max": max_cpu_pressure,
            "mem_effective_gb": effective, "mem_required_gb": required,
            "mem_reserve_gb": reserve, "mem_source": (
                "cgroup" if cg["limited"] else "host" if host_mem is not None else None),
            "mem_host_available_gb": host_mem,
            "mem_cgroup_available": cg["available"], "mem_cgroup_limited": cg["limited"],
            "mem_cgroup_path": cg["path"], "mem_cgroup_limit_gb": cg["limit_gb"],
            "mem_cgroup_current_gb": cg["current_gb"],
            "mem_cgroup_committed_gb": cg["committed_gb"],
            "mem_cgroup_headroom_gb": cg["headroom_gb"], "mem_cgroup_peak_gb": cg["peak_gb"],
            "mem_cgroup_used_pct": cg["used_pct"], "mem_nested_docker_gb": cg["nested_gb"],
            "mem_pressure_avg10": cg["pressure"], "mem_pressure_max": max_pressure,
            "mem_near_ceiling_pct": near_ceiling,
            "disk_free_gb": disk, "disk_min_gb": min_disk,
            "stacks": stacks, "stacks_max": max_stacks, "docker_available": stacks is not None,
            "processes": processes, "processes_max": max_processes,
            "oom_events": {k: v for k, v in cg["oom"].items() if k in ("max", "oom", "oom_kill")},
            "pids": pids(cgroup_root),
        },
    }


def verdict_line(r):
    m = r["metrics"]
    if m.get("cpu_pressure_avg10") is not None:
        cpu_part = (f"cpu {fmt(m['cpu_pressure_avg10'])}% stall "
                    f"(max {m['cpu_pressure_max']:g})")
    else:
        cpu_part = (f"load {fmt(m['load_per_core'])}/core "
                    f"(max {m['load_max']:g})")
    parts = [
        f"need {r['need']}",
        cpu_part,
        f"mem {fmt(m['mem_effective_gb'])}G effective of "
        f"{fmt(m['mem_cgroup_headroom_gb'])}G cgroup / "
        f"{fmt(m['mem_host_available_gb'])}G host "
        f"(need {m['mem_required_gb']:g}, reserve {m['mem_reserve_gb']:g})",
        f"disk {fmt(m['disk_free_gb'])}G (min {m['disk_min_gb']:g})",
        f"stacks {m['stacks'] if m['stacks'] is not None else 'n/a'}+1 "
        f"(max {m['stacks_max']:g})",
        f"processes {m['processes'] if m['processes'] is not None else 'n/a'} "
        f"(max {m['processes_max']:g})",
        f"pressure {fmt(m['mem_pressure_avg10'])}% (max {m['mem_pressure_max']:g})",
    ]
    detail = []
    if m.get("cpu_pressure_avg10") is not None and m["load_per_core"] is not None:
        # report the unscoped number too, so the reader can tell a shared
        # host's tenants apart from a cgroup that is out of room
        detail.append(f"host load {fmt(m['load_per_core'])}/core unscoped")
    if m["mem_cgroup_limited"]:
        detail.append(f"cgroup {m['mem_cgroup_path']} limit "
                      f"{fmt(m['mem_cgroup_limit_gb'])}G "
                      f"committed {fmt(m['mem_cgroup_committed_gb'])}G "
                      f"current {fmt(m['mem_cgroup_current_gb'])}G")
    else:
        detail.append("cgroup unlimited" if m["mem_cgroup_available"] else "cgroup n/a")
    if m["mem_nested_docker_gb"] is not None:
        detail.append(f"docker {fmt(m['mem_nested_docker_gb'])}G of it")
    if m["mem_cgroup_peak_gb"] is not None:
        detail.append(f"peak {fmt(m['mem_cgroup_peak_gb'])}G")
    oom = m["oom_events"]
    if oom:
        detail.append(f"oom {oom.get('oom', 0)}/{oom.get('oom_kill', 0)} kill")
    if m["pids"] and m["pids"].get("max"):
        detail.append(f"pids {m['pids']['current']}/{m['pids']['max']}")
    if m["stacks"] is None:
        detail.append("docker unavailable")
    return ("agent_capacity: " + ", ".join(parts) + " [" + "; ".join(detail) + "] -> "
            + ("BUSY: " + ",".join(r["reasons"]) if r["reasons"] else "fits"))


def main(argv=None):
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="agent_capacity", description=__doc__.splitlines()[0],
        epilog="exit 0 fits, 1 busy, 2 usage")
    ap.add_argument("--need", choices=("stack", "suite", "process", "none"),
                    default="stack",
                    help="class of the job about to start (default stack)")
    ap.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="print the verdict as one JSON object")
    ap.add_argument("--stacks", type=int, default=None,
                    help="stacks already running, instead of counting with docker ps")
    ap.add_argument("--processes", type=int, default=None,
                    help="agent processes already running, instead of scanning /proc")
    ap.add_argument("--wait", type=int, default=0, metavar="SECONDS",
                    help="queue for up to SECONDS seconds for a refusal to clear "
                         "before exiting busy; progress goes to stderr")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    def ask():
        return evaluate(need=args.need, stacks=args.stacks, processes=args.processes)

    result = ask()
    deadline = time.monotonic() + max(0, args.wait)
    while not result["fits"] and time.monotonic() < deadline:
        # A refusal clears on its own - another job finishing, a charge being
        # reclaimed - so queueing for room is legitimate waiting, while
        # displacing a running job is not. Progress goes to stderr, so a
        # --json caller still reads exactly one object on stdout.
        left = deadline - time.monotonic()
        print(f"agent_capacity: waiting {int(left)}s for "
              + ",".join(result["reasons"]), file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
        time.sleep(min(15.0, max(0.0, left)))
        result = ask()
    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps(result))
    else:
        print(verdict_line(result))
    return 1 if not result["fits"] else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
